th the largest y-coordinate) and the bottommost point.\n5. Draw horizontal lines through these two points. The vertical distance between these horizontal lines must be at most 1 unit for similar reasons as stated above.\n6. These four lines (two vertical and two horizontal) form a bounding rectangle. This new rectangle will have sides parallel to the coordinate axes.\n7. The dimensions of this rectangle will not exceed the dimensions of the rectangle given in the problem statement.\n8. Since any three points can be translated into the rectangle with the vertices \\((0,0), (1,0), (0,2), (1,2)\\), this bounding rectangle identified must be able to fit entirely within this given rectangle by a parallel translation.\n\nThus, it implies that the complete set of points can also be translated into the target rectangle through one parallel translation.\n   \n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Yes, it is possible to move all the points into the rectangle with a single parallel translation.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Place five 1s, five 2s, five 3s, five 4s, and five 5s into a 5x5 grid (one number in each cell) such that the absolute difference between any two numbers in the same column does not exceed 2. Considering the sum of the numbers in each column, let the minimum value of these five sums be $M$. What is the maximum value of $M$?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to tackle this problem. So, the task is to place five 1s, five 2s, five 3s, five 4s, and five 5s into a 5x5 grid. Each column must have numbers where the absolute difference between any two numbers in the same column doesn't exceed 2. Then, we need to find the maximum possible minimum column sum M. Hmm, that sounds a bit complicated, but let me break it down.\n\nFirst, let's understand the constraints. Each number from 1 to 5 appears exactly five times, one in each row and column? Wait, no, actually, the problem just says