1/b in S, k(a -1/b) +c is still in S. However, this is restrictive.\n\nSuppose we try f(x)=x/2 and g(x)=x +1. These are strictly increasing. But do they map S to S?\n\nFor f(x)=x/2: take x=a -1/b. Then x/2=(a/2) -1/(2b). But a/2 must be an integer minus 1/(2b). If a is even, then a/2 is integer, but 1/(2b) is not of the form 1/c for c \u2208 \u2115. If a is odd, a/2 is not an integer. Thus, f(x)=x/2 does not map S to S.\n\nSimilarly, g(x)=x +1 maps S to S as previously discussed. But f(x)=x +c maps S to S if c is an integer. But if c is non-integer, it might not.\n\nAlternatively, define f(x)=x +1/2. Then f(a -1/b)=a -1/b +1/2. But this equals (a) + (1/2 -1/b). To be in S, this must be expressible as c -1/d. Suppose b=2: x=a -1/2. Then f(x)=a -1/2 +1/2=a. Which is an integer, but S contains integers (when b=1: a -1/1=a -1). Wait, a is an integer, but a itself is not in S unless expressed as (a +1) -1/1. For example, 1 is in S as 2 -1/1=1. So if f(x)=a -1/b +1/2, this can be written as (a) + (1/2 -1/b). If 1/2 -1/b is negative, which occurs when b=1: 1/2 -1= -1/2, so f(x)=a -1/2, which is in S as (a -1) +1 -1/2=(a -1) +1/2. Wait, no. a -1/2 is directly in S: (a) -1/2, which requires a \u22651. So yes, a -1/2 is in S. Similarly, for b \u22652, 1/2 -1/b is positive but not of the form 1/d. For example, if b=3: 1/2 -1/3=1/6. So f(x)=a -1/3 +1/2=a +1/6. This is not in S because it's a +1/6, which cannot be expressed as c -1/d for c \u2208 \u2115, d \u2208 \u2115. Because c must be at least 1, and c -1/d =a +1/6 \u21d2c =a +1/6 +1/d. But c must be an integer, and 1/d must cancel the 1/6. But 1/d= integer -a -1/6. This is impossible. Therefore, f(x)=x +1/2 does not map S to S.\n\nHence, affine functions are not suitable. \n\nAnother idea: Use functions that manipulate the a and b components separately. For example, define f(a -1/b)= (a) -1/(b+1) and g(a -1/b)= (a +1) -1/(b). Then:\n\nCompute f(g(g(x))):\n\nx =a -1/b.\n\ng(x)= (a +1) -1/b.\n\ng(g(x))= (a +2) -1/b.\n\nf(g(g(