g(x) \\geq x$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n   - We claim that $g_k(x) \\leq f(x)$ for all $k \\geq 0$ and all positive integers $x$. We will prove this claim by induction on $k$.\n   \n3. **Base case (k = 0):** \n   - For $k = 0$, we have $g_0(x) = x \\leq f(x)$ (which is trivially true).\n\n4. **Induction Hypothesis:** \n   - Assume that $g_k(x) \\leq f(x)$ holds for some $k \\geq 0$.\n\n5. **Induction Step:**\n   - Consider $g_{k+1}(x) = g(g_k(x))$.\n   - By the induction hypothesis, we know $g_k(x) \\leq f(x)$.\n   - Because $g$ is strictly increasing, applying $g$ to both sides of the inequality $g_k(x) \\leq f(x)$ gives:\n     \\[\n     g(g_k(x)) \\leq g(f(x))\n     \\]\n   - According to condition (ii), $f(g(g(x))) < g(f(x))$. If we apply this repeatedly, we get:\n     \\[\n     g_{k+1}(x) = g(g_k(x)) \\leq g(f(x))\n     \\]\n   - Since $f$ and $g$ are strictly increasing and $g(f(x)) > f(g(g(x)))$, it follows that:\n     \\[\n     g(g_k(x)) < f(x)\n     \\]\n   - Therefore:\n     \\[\n     g_{k+1}(x) < f(x)\n     \\]\n   - So by induction, $g_k(x) \\leq f(x)$ for all $k \\geq 0$.\n\n6. **Contradiction:**\n   - If $g(x) = x$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{N}$, then $f(g(g(x))) = f(x) = g(f(x))$, a contradiction with condition (ii).\n   - Therefore, there must exist an $x_0 \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that $x_0 < g(x_0)$.\n   - Consider the sequence $x_0, x_1, \\ldots$ where $x_k = g_k(x_0)$.\n   - The sequence is increasing. This is because $x_0 < g(x_0) = x_1$, and if $x_k < x_{k+1}$, then $x_{k+1} = g(x_k)$, so $x_{k+1} < g(x_{k+1}) = x_{k+2}$.\n   - Thus, we have $x_0 < x_1 < \\cdots$, an increasing sequence of positive integers bounded above by $f(x_0)$ which contradicts the unbounded nature of $\\mathbb{N}$.\n\n**Conclusion for part (a):** No Spanish Couple exists on $\\mathbb{N}$.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nFor part (a):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Part (b):**\n\n1. **Set Definition:**\n   - Consider the set $S = \\left\\{ \\frac{a-1}{b} : a, b \\in \