he angles of the spherical triangle are equal to the dihedral angles. \n\nThe spherical law of cosines is:\n\ncos \u03b3 = cos \u03b1 cos \u03b2 + sin \u03b1 sin \u03b2 cos \u03b8\n\nWhere \u03b3 is the side opposite to the angle \u03b8 in the spherical triangle. Rearranging:\n\ncos \u03b8 = (cos \u03b3 - cos \u03b1 cos \u03b2) / (sin \u03b1 sin \u03b2)\n\nWhich is the formula we used. Therefore, in our case, \u03b3 is the angle between SA and SB, which is \u03c0/2. Therefore:\n\ncos \u03b8 = (cos \u03c0/2 - cos \u03b1 cos \u03b2) / (sin \u03b1 sin \u03b2) = -cot \u03b1 cot \u03b2\n\nThus, \u03b8 = \u03c0 - arccos(cot \u03b1 cot \u03b2), as required. \n\nThis confirms the result. So the dihedral angle \u03b8 is indeed \u03c0 minus the arccos of (cot \u03b1 cot \u03b2). \n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{\\theta = \\pi - \\arccos(\\cot \\alpha \\cot \\beta)}", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that the dihedral angle \\(\\theta\\) along edge \\(SC\\) in tetrahedron \\(SABC\\) is given by \\(\\theta = \\pi - \\arccos(\\cot \\alpha \\cot \\beta)\\), we start by considering the given angles at vertex \\(S\\): \\(\\angle ASB = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), \\(\\angle ASC = \\alpha\\), and \\(\\angle BSC = \\beta\\). \n\nWe use the formula relating the dihedral angle to the face angles at a vertex. For a trihedron with face angles \\(\\alpha\\), \\(\\beta\\), and \\(\\gamma\\) (the angle between edges not lying on the dihedral angle), the dihedral angle \\(\\theta\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\cos \\theta = \\frac{\\cos \\gamma - \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta}\n\\]\n\nIn our case, the face angles are \\(\\alpha\\) (between \\(SA\\) and \\(SC\\)), \\(\\beta\\) (between \\(SB\\) and \\(SC\\)), and \\(\\gamma = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) (between \\(SA\\) and \\(SB\\)). Plugging these into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\cos \\theta = \\frac{\\cos \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\cos \\frac{\\pi}{2} = 0\\), this simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\cos \\theta = \\frac{0 - \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta} = -\\cot \\alpha \\cot \\beta\n\\]\n\nThus, the angle \\(\\theta\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\theta = \\arccos(-\\cot \\alpha \\cot \\beta)\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\arccos(-x) = \\pi - \\arccos(x)\\) for \\(x\\) in \\([-1, 1]\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\theta = \\pi - \\arccos(\\cot \\alpha \\cot \\beta)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the dihedral angle \\(\\theta\\) along edge \\(SC\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\theta = \\pi - \\arccos(\\cot \\alpha \\cot \\beta)}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. The problem is to prove that the plane angle \\(\\theta\\) between the face planes \\(ASC\\) and \\(BSC\\) in a tetrahedron \\(SABC\\) can be expressed as \\(\\theta = \\pi - \\arccos(\\cot \\alpha \\cot \\beta)\\). Given:\n    - \\(\\angle ASB = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\)\n    - \\(\\angle ASC = \\alpha\\) such that \\(\\theta < \\alpha < \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\)\n    - \\(\\angle BSC = \\beta\\) such that \\(0 < \\beta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\)\n\n2. Consider the line \\(SC\\) along which we need to find the plane angle \\(\\theta\\). \n\n3. Construct a point \\(D\\) on \\(SC\\) such that \\(SD = 1\\).\n\n4. Through point \\(D\\), we can construct perpendicular lines to the planes \\(ASC\\) and \\(BSC\\) which intersect \\(SA\\) at \\(E\\) and \\(SB\\) at \\(F\\), respectively.\n\n5. In the right triangle \\(\\triangle ESD\\):\n    - \\(ED = \\tan \\alpha\\)\n    - \\(ES = \\sec \\alpha\\)\n\n6. In the right triangle \\(\\triangle FSD\\):\n    - \\(FD = \\tan \\beta\\)\n    - \\(FS = \\sec \\beta\\)\n\n7. Now consider the right triangle \\(\\triangle EFS\\):\n\n    - Using the Pythagorean theorem in \\(\\triangle EFS\\), we get:\n      \\[\n      EF^2 = ES^2 + FS^2 = \\sec^2 \\alpha + \\sec^2 \\beta\n      \\]\n\n8. In the right triangle \\(\\triangle EDF\\):\n\n    - The cosine of the angle \\(\\theta\\) can be determined using the law of cosines:\n      \\[\n      \\cos \\theta = \\frac{ED^2 + FD^2 - EF^2}{2 \\cdot ED \\cdot FD}\n    