n   - For $x = -1$:\n     \\[\n     Q(-1) = (a(-1) + b)(-1-2)(-1-3)(-1-4)(-1-5) = (-a + b) (-3)(-4)(-5)(-6)\n     \\]\n     Simplifying,\n     \\[\n     Q(-1) = (-a + b) (360)\n     \\]\n     We know:\n     \\[\n     Q(-1) = 720 \\implies 360(-a + b) = 720 \\implies -a + b = 2\n     \\]\n\n6. Solve the system of linear equations:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   a + b = -30 \\\\\n   -a + b = 2\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   Adding the equations:\n   \\[\n   2b = -28 \\implies b = -14\n   \\]\n   Subtracting the first equation from the second:\n   \\[\n   2a = -32 \\implies a = -16\n   \\]\n\n7. Substitute $a = -16$ and $b = -14$ back into $Q(x)$:\n   \\[\n   Q(x) = (-16x - 14)(x-2)(x-3)(x-4)(x-5)\n   \\]\n\n8. Given the relation $Q(x) = (x^2 - 1) P(x) - 720x$, solve for $P(6)$:\n   \\[\n   (6^2 - 1) P(6) - 720 \\cdot 6 = Q(6)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   35 P(6) - 4320 = (-16 \\cdot 6 - 14)(6-2)(6-3)(6-4)(6-5)\n   \\]\n   Simplify the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   = (-110)(4)(3)(2)(1) = -110 \\cdot 24 = -2640\n   \\]\n   Thus,\n   \\[\n   35 P(6) - 4320 = -2640 \\implies 35 P(6) = -2640 + 4320 = 1680\n   \\]\n   Solving for $P(6)$:\n   \\[\n   P(6) = \\frac{1680}{35} = 48\n   \\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{48}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the integral $\\int \\sin^{4} x \\cos^{2} x \\, dx$.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the integral of sin^4(x) cos^2(x) dx. Hmm, integrals with powers of sine and cosine can be tricky. Let me think about the methods I know. Maybe using trigonometric identities to simplify the expression before integrating?\n\nI remember that for even powers of sine and cosine, it's often helpful to use power-reduction formulas. The power-reduction identities come from the double-angle formulas. Let me recall them. The basic ones are:\n\nsin^2(x) = (1 - cos(2x))/2\n\ncos^2(x) = (1 + cos(2x))/2\n\nBut here I have sin^4(x) and cos^2(x). Maybe I can break down sin^4(x