nd by adding them up. If their total length is more than 2, maybe the resultant is more than 1? Not sure.\n\nWait, let's consider this: If all vectors are in a semicircle, then their sum can be maximized by aligning them all in the same direction. But if they are spread out over the semicircle, the resultant would be somewhere in between. Maybe use the concept of covering the plane with sectors and using the pigeonhole principle to find a sector with enough vectors.\n\nAlternatively, here's a standard result: For any set of vectors in the plane with total length S, there exists a subset whose sum has length at least S/(2\u03c0). Hmm, if S=4, then 4/(2\u03c0) \u2248 0.636, which is less than 1, so that might not help.\n\nWait, but maybe a better bound exists. Alternatively, if you consider that in the worst case, the vectors are arranged to cancel each other as much as possible. For example, if they are arranged in pairs pointing in opposite directions. Then, as I considered earlier, each pair cancels. But even in that case, if the total length is 4, there are two such pairs. If you take one vector from each pair, depending on their directions, you might be able to get a sum greater than 1.\n\nWait, but in the case of two pairs of opposite vectors: each pair has two vectors of length 1 in opposite directions. So total length is 4. If I take one vector from each pair, the resultant would depend on the angle between the two vectors. If they are at 180 degrees, then they cancel, but if they are at 0 degrees, they add. But in reality, they can be at any angle. However, the problem states that the original vectors can be in any directions. So maybe the worst case is when the vectors are arranged to minimize the maximum subset sum. So we need to show that even in that case, the maximum is still greater than 1.\n\nAlternatively, think of the problem as an optimization: minimize the maximum subset sum over all possible vector arrangements with total length 4. If the minimal 