quence modulo m is purely periodic.\n\nTherefore, since the sequence is purely periodic, it must return to the initial pair (0,1) after \u03c0(m) terms. Therefore, F\u2080 \u22610 mod m, F\u2081\u22611 mod m, F_{\u03c0(m)}\u22610 mod m, F_{\u03c0(m)+1}\u22611 mod m, etc. Therefore, F_{k\u03c0(m)} \u22610 mod m for all k \u22650, which are infinitely many terms. Hence, the result follows.\n\nWait, so even in the case of m=5, even though there are zeros at 0,5,10,15,... which are every 5 terms, the Pisano period is 20, but F_{k\u03c0(m)} = F_{20k} would be 0 mod5. However, in reality, the zeros at 0,5,10,15,20,... include terms like F\u2085, F\u2081\u2080, etc., which are not multiples of the Pisano period. So perhaps my initial reasoning is conflicting with the example.\n\nWait, no. In the example with m=5, the Pisano period is 20. Therefore, F_{20} \u22610 mod5, F_{40}\u22610 mod5, etc., but also F\u2085\u22610 mod5, F\u2081\u2080\u22610 mod5, etc. Therefore, there are multiple arithmetic progressions where zeros occur. Therefore, in addition to the zeros at k\u03c0(m), which are 0,20,40,..., there are zeros at 5,10,15,25, etc. So the presence of one zero ensures infinitely many through periodicity, but there might be multiple \"sources\" of zeros.\n\nBut the key point is that even if there are multiple zeros within a Pisano period, as long as there is at least one zero, which there is because the Pisano period brings back the initial pair (0,1), hence F\u2080\u22610 mod m, then periodicity ensures that this zero repeats every \u03c0(m) terms. Therefore, F\u2080, F_{\u03c0(m)}, F_{2\u03c0(m)}, etc., are all zeros modulo m. Therefore, regardless of other zeros that may appear within the period, these terms at multiples of \u03c0(m) are zeros, hence there are infinitely many.\n\nTherefore, the crucial steps are:\n\n1. The Fibonacci sequence modulo m is purely periodic with period \u03c0(m). This is due to the finiteness of possible pairs modulo m and the recurrence relation.\n\n2. The initial pair (0,1) must recur after \u03c0(m) terms, hence F_{\u03c0(m)} \u22610 mod m and F_{\u03c0(m)+1}\u22611 mod m.\n\n3. Therefore, F_{k\u03c0(m)} \u22610 mod m for all integers k \u22650, which are infinitely many terms.\n\nThus, there are infinitely many Fibonacci numbers divisible by m.\n\nAlternatively, even without referencing the Pisano period, we can argue as follows:\n\nConsider the Fibonacci sequence modulo m. Since there are only m\u00b2 possible pairs of residues (F\u2099, F\u2099\u208a\u2081) modulo m, by the Pigeonhole Principle, there must exist indices i < j such that (F_i, F_{i+1}) \u2261 (F_j, F_{j+1}) mod m. Then, by the recurrence relation, the sequences starting at i and j will be identical modulo m. Therefore, the sequence is periodic from i onward with period j - i. However, because the Fibonacci sequence is determined by its initial conditions, the periodicity must extend back to the start, making the entire sequence periodic with period j - i. Hence, the Fibonacci sequence modulo m is purely periodic.\n\nSince the sequence is purely periodic, it must return to the initial pair (0,1). Let the period be \u03c0. Then, F\u2080 \u22610 mod m, F_\u03c0 \u22610 mod m, F_{2\u03c0}\u22610 mod m, etc., leading to infinitely many terms divisible by m.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the value of m, the periodicity ensures that 0 reappears every \u03c0(m) terms, hence there are infinitely many Fibonacci numbers divisible by m.\n\nBut to make this rigorous, we should formalize the argument.\n\nAnother approach: suppose we fix m and consider the Fibonacci sequence modulo m. There are m\u00b2 possible pairs (a, b) of residues modulo m. By the Pigeonhole Principle, the sequence of pairs (F\u2080, F\u2081), (F\u2081, F\u2082), ..., (F_{m\u00b2}, F_{m\u00b2 +1})} must contain a repeated pair. Suppose the pairs at positions i and j are equal, where i < j \u2264 m\u00b2. Then, by the recurrence relation, the sequences starting at i and j must