ide: 4S\u00b2 -12S +9 >= S\u00b2\n\nSubtract S\u00b2: 3S\u00b2 -12S +9 >=0\n\nDivide by 3: S\u00b2 -4S +3 >=0\n\nFactor: (S -1)(S -3) >=0\n\nSo, this inequality holds when S <=1 or S >=3. But since x, y, z >0 and xyz=1, by AM-GM, S >=3*(xyz)^{1/3}=3. So S >=3. Therefore, (S -1)(S -3) >=0 holds since S >=3, so S -3 >=0, hence product >=0.\n\nTherefore, Titu's lemma gives us LHS >= [2S -3]^2 / S >= S, since [2S -3]^2 / S >= S.\n\nBut wait, we have shown that LHS >= [2S -3]^2 / S, and that [2S -3]^2 / S >= S. Therefore, combining these, LHS >= S, which is exactly what we need to prove. Therefore, the inequality holds.\n\nWait, so this seems like a valid approach? Let me check again.\n\nStarting with Titu's lemma (Cauchy-Schwarz inequality in the form of sum a_i^2 / b_i >= (sum a_i)^2 / sum b_i):\n\nSum [(x + y -1)^2 / z] >= [sum (x + y -1)]\u00b2 / (z + x + y)\n\nSum (x + y -1) = (x + y -1) + (y + z -1) + (z + x -1) = 2x + 2y + 2z -3\n\nTherefore, LHS >= [2(x + y + z) -3]^2 / (x + y + z)\n\nThen, as we showed, since xyz =1, AM-GM gives x + y + z >=3, so S >=3. Then [2S -3]^2 / S >= S.\n\nBecause, let's verify:\n\n[2S -3]^2 / S >= S\n\nMultiply both sides by S (positive):\n\n(2S -3)^2 >= S\u00b2\n\nWhich gives 4S\u00b2 -12S +9 >= S\u00b2 => 3S\u00b2 -12S +9 >=0 => S\u00b2 -4S +3 >=0 => (S -1)(S -3) >=0\n\nSince S >=3, S -3 >=0, so yes, (S -1)(S -3) >=0. Therefore, this inequality holds.\n\nTherefore, combining the two results:\n\nSum [(x + y -1)^2 / z] >= [2S -3]^2 / S >= S = x + y + z\n\nTherefore, the original inequality holds.\n\nWait, so is this a valid proof? Let me make sure each step is correct.\n\nFirst, Titu's lemma: yes, applied correctly. The sum of (a_i)^2 / b_i >= (sum a_i)^2 / sum b_i. Here, a_i = (x + y -1), (y + z -1), (z + x -1), and b_i = z, x, y. So yes, that's correctly applied.\n\nThen, sum a_i = 2(x + y + z) -3, correct.\n\nThen, sum b_i = z + x + y = x + y + z, correct.\n\nThen, we have LHS >= [2S -3]^2 / S, where S = x + y + z.\n\nThen, since by 