lks \\( 10 \\, \\text{km} \\) in 2 hours (arrives at 3 hours).  \n     - B and C take \\( \\approx 2.84 \\, \\text{hours} \\) (arrive early).  \n   - For \\( t = 1.1 \\, \\text{hours} \\):  \n     - A walks \\( 5 \\, \\text{km} \\) in 1 hour (arrives at \\( 2.1 \\, \\text{hours} \\)).  \n     - B and C take exactly 3 hours.  \n\n**Conclusion:**  \nBy choosing \\( t \\) between 1 and 1.1 hours, all three geologists arrive at the station within 3 hours. The motorcycle shuttling strategy ensures timely arrival.\n\n**Answer:**  \nYes, they can reach the station within 3 hours.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Identify the distances and speeds involved:**\n   - The total distance to the station is \\(60\\, \\text{km}\\).\n   - The motorcycle can carry two people at a maximum speed of \\(50\\, \\text{km/h}\\).\n   - A pedestrian walks at a speed of \\(5\\, \\text{km/h}\\).\n   \n2. **Plan the trip:**\n   - Two of the geologists will ride the motorcycle for the first part of the journey.\n   - The third geologist will walk in the same direction.\n   \n3. **First phase of the journey (1 hour):**\n   - The motorcycle travels for \\(1\\) hour at \\(50\\, \\text{km/h}\\):\n     \\[\n     \\text{Distance covered by motorcycle} = 50\\, \\text{km}\n     \\]\n   - The walking geologist covers a distance of:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Distance covered by pedestrian} = 5\\, \\text{km}\n     \\]\n   \n4. **Adjust the positions after 1 hour:**\n   - The two geologists on the motorcycle are now \\(50\\, \\text{km}\\) away from the base, which leaves \\(10\\, \\text{km}\\) to the station.\n   - The pedestrian is \\(45\\, \\text{km}\\) behind them:\n     \\[\n     50\\, \\text{km} - 5\\, \\text{km} = 45\\, \\text{km}\n     \\]\n   \n5. **Sending one geologist on foot:**\n   - One geologist on the motorcycle continues on foot to cover the remaining \\(10\\, \\text{km}\\) to the station. As they walk at \\(5\\, \\text{km/h}\\), they will take:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Time to walk } 10\\, \\text{km} = \\frac{10\\, \\text{km}}{5\\, \\text{km/h}} = 2\\, \\text{hours}\n     \\]\n   \n6. **Second phase of the journey for the motorcycle:**\n   - The other geologist on the motorcycle turns back to pick up the walking geologist.\n   - The separation between the motorcycle and the pedestrian is \\(45\\, \\text{km}\\).\n   - They are moving towards each other with combined speed:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Combined speed} = 50\\, \\text{km/h} + 5\\, \\text{km/h} = 55\\, \\text{km/h}\n     \\]\n   - Time to meet the pedestrian:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Time to meet} = \\frac{45\\, \\text{km}}{55\\, \\text{km/h}} = \\frac{9}{11}\\, \\text{hours}\n     \\]\n   \n7. **Distance covered by the motorcycle during this time:**\n   - Distance covered by the motorcycle while meeting the pedestrian:\n     \\[\n     50\\, \\text{km/h} \\times \\frac{9}{11}\\, \\text{hours} = \\frac{450}{11}\\, \\text{km}\n     \\]\n   \n8. **Remaining distance for the motorcycle:**\n   - Total distance from the meeting point to the station:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Total distance} = \\frac{450\\, \\text{km}}{11} + 10\\, \\text{km} = \\frac{560}{11}\\, \\text{km}\n     \\]\n   \n9. **Time taken to cover the remaining distance:**\n   - Time to reach the station from the meeting point:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Time} = \\frac{560}{11} \\, \\text{km} \\div 50 \\, \\text{km/h} = \\frac{56}{55}\\, \\text{hours}\n     \\]\n   \n10. **Total time for the second geologist:**\n    - Total time from the departure to the station:\n      \\[\n      \\text{Total time} = 1\\, \\text{hour} + \\frac{56}{55}\\, \\text{hours} + \\frac{9}{11}\\, \\text{hours} = 1 + \\frac{56}{55} + \\frac{45}{55} = 2 \\frac{46}{55} \\text{ hours}\n      \\]\n   \n### Conclusion:\nSince \\(2 \\frac{46}{55}\\, \\text{hours} \\approx 2.836 \\, \\text{hours} < 3 \\, \\text{hours}\\), the geologists can indeed reach the station within 3 hours.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "sta