d (5,7). So that's three infected neighbors. Therefore, (4,6) would get infected in the next step. Similarly, all cells adjacent to the 3x3 block would have three infected neighbors, so they would get infected. Then, the next layer out would have cells adjacent to the now-infected perimeter. Those cells would have neighbors from the perimeter. For example, the cell at (3,6) is adjacent to (4,5), (4,6), (4,7). If (4,5), (4,6), (4,7) are infected, then (3,6) is adjacent to three infected cells. So (3,6) would get infected. Similarly, all cells adjacent to the perimeter would get infected in the subsequent steps. This seems like a spreading pattern where each layer gets infected one after another. If this continues, eventually the entire grid would be infected.\n\nBut wait, the grid is 10x10. Let me check the boundaries. The initial 3x3 block is in the center. As the infection spreads outward, each concentric layer would get infected. However, when the infection reaches the edge of the grid, those edge cells only have neighbors on one side. For example, the cell at (1,6) is on the top edge. Its neighbors are (2,6), (1,5), (1,7). When the infection spreads to (2,6), (1,5), and (1,7), but if (1,5) and (1,7) are not yet infected, then (1,6) would need two infected neighbors. But if (2,6) is infected, then (1,6) only has one infected neighbor. Hmm, so maybe the edges might not get infected?\n\nWait, let's think again. If the infection spreads layer by layer from the center, each layer being a ring around the previous one. When the infection reaches the edge, the outermost ring would have cells that are on the grid's boundary. For example, when the infection is at layer 4 (assuming the center is layer 0), the cells at distance 4 from the center. But if the grid is 10x10, the distance from the center to the edge is 4 or 5 cells? Let's see, the grid is 10x10, so the center would be around (5,5) to (6,6). The distance from the center to the top edge is 5 cells (rows 5 to 10, but starting from row 5, moving up 5 rows gets to row 10? Wait, maybe coordinates are from 1 to 10. So if the center is at (5.5,5.5), but since we have discrete cells, maybe the center is considered as (5,5) to (6,6). Anyway, the exact distance might not matter. The point is, when the infection spreads outward, each new layer requires the previous layer to be fully infected so that the next layer can have two infected neighbors.\n\nBut when approaching the edge, the cells on the edge only have three neighbors (for corner cells) or five neighbors (for edge cells not in the corner). Wait, no. Each cell has up to four neighbors: up, down, left, right. So edge cells have three neighbors, and corner cells have two.\n\nSo, for example, the cell at (1,1) has neighbors (1,2) and (2,1). If we are trying to infect the corner cell (1,1), it needs two infected neighbors. But (1,2) and (2,1) would need to be infected first. However, if those cells are part of the spreading wave, then they might get infected in the previous steps. Let's see.\n\nSuppose the infection spreads outward in concentric squares. Starting from the 3x3 block, the next layer would be a 5x5 block, then 7x7, then 9x9, and finally 10x10. Wait, but 9x9 is still one cell away from the edge. The next layer would attempt to go to 11x11, but the grid is only 10x10. So the last layer would be incomplete.\n\nWait, perhaps the way the infection spreads is that each layer adds a ring around the previous infected area. If the initial infected area is a 3x3 square, the next step infects the surrounding cells, making a 5x5 square. Then next step, 7x7, then 9x9, then the entire 10x10. Let me check:\n\n- Step 0: 3x3 infected.\n\n- Step 1: All cells adjacent to the 3x3 become infected. Since the 3x3 is surrounded by a layer of cells, each of those cells has at least two infected neighbors (they are adjacent to two cells in the 3x3). Wait, no. If you have a 3x3 block, the cells adjacent to it on the outside would each be adjacent to three cells in the 3x3 block. For example, the cell above the center of the 3x3 block is adjacent to three infected cells. So all surrounding cells would get infected, turning the 3x3 into a 5x5.\n\nWait, but 3x3 plus one layer would be 5x5. Then the next layer would be 7x7, then 9x9, then 11x11. But the grid is only 10x10, so the last layer would be 10x10. Let's calculate how many steps it takes. The initial 3x3 is at the center. Each step adds a layer. The distance from the center to the edge is 4.5 cells (since 10/2 = 5, so from center at 5.5 to edge is 4.5 cells). But since we're dealing with discrete cells, maybe it takes 5 steps. Wait, perhaps not. Let's see:\n\nStarting with 3x3 (distance 1 from center), then 5x5 (distance 2), 7x7 (distance 3), 9x9 (distance 4), and 10x10 (distance 5). But the 10x10 grid would require the infection to reach the edges. However, when trying to infect the last layer, the cells on the edge might not have two infected neighbors because they are on the edge. For example, the cell at (10,10) is a corner. To infect it, it needs two infected neighbors. Its neighbors are (9,10) and (10,9). So both (9,10) and (10,9) need to be infected first. Then, (10,10) would have two infected neighbors and would become infected. Similarly, other edge cells would need their adjacent cells to be infected.\n\nSo if the infection spreads as a square, each time expanding outward by one layer, then when it reaches the edge, the edge cells would still get infected because their inward neighbors would have been infected in the previous step. For example, to infect (10,10), you need (9,10) and (10,9). Those would be part of the previous layer. If the previous layer included the 9th row and 9th column, then (9,10) and (10,9) would be infected, allowing (10,10) to get infected. So maybe starting with a 3x3 block can indeed infect the entire grid.\n\nBut wait, in our case, we start with nine cells arranged in a 3x3 block. Then, in the first step, the perimeter around the 3x3 becomes infected, making a 5x5 block. Then the next perimeter becomes a 7x7, then 9x9, and finally 10x10. However, the step from 9x9 to 10x10 requires infecting the outermost layer. The 9x9 block is from (1,1) to (9,9). The next layer would be adding the 10th row and column. Each cell in the 10th row (from (10,1) to (10,10)) is adjacent to cells in the 9th row. Similarly, the 10th column is adjacent to the 9th column. So, for example, cell (10,1) is adjacent to (9,1). But (9,1) is part of the 9x9 block. However, (10,1) only has one infected neighbor, (9,1), unless (10,2) is also infected. Wait, no. If we are expanding from 9x9 to 10x10, the cells in the 10th row and 10th column need to be infected. Each of these cells has only one neighbor in the 9th row or 9th column. For example, (10,5) is adjacent only to (9,5). So if only (9,5) is infected, (10,5) has only one infected neighbor and won't get infected. Therefore, this method fails at the edges.\n\nHmm, so starting with a 3x3 block in the center might not infect the entire grid because the edge cells only have one infected neighbor. Therefore, the infection would stop at the 9x9 block. But the grid is 10x10, so we need to infect those last cells on the 10th row and column. How can that happen?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the initial infected cells need to be arranged not in a solid block but in a different pattern that can spread to the edges. For example, if the initial infected cells are placed in such a way that they can create two separate \"fronts\" that meet at the edges.\n\nWait, another idea. If the initial infected cells are arranged along a diagonal, or in a way that they can spread in both row and column directions. For example, if we have infected cells spaced out such that they can form two adjacent infected cells along the edges, allowing the edge cells to get infected.\n\nBut with only nine cells, it's challenging. Let me think. Suppose instead of a 3x3 block, we arrange the nine cells as two separate lines or crosses. For example, a horizontal line and a vertical line intersecting. But how would that help?\n\nAlternatively, maybe using a \"seed\" pattern that can spread in all four directions. For example, placing infected cells in a way that they can grow arms that expand outward. However, to form an arm, you need to have each new cell added by having two infected neighbors. So, for example, a line of infected cells can only grow if the tip has two infected neighbors. Wait, if you have a straight line of infected cells, the next cell in the line can be infected because it has one infected neighbor (the previous cell in the line), but it needs another. So unless there's a branch or another adjacent infected cell, the line can't grow.\n\nTherefore, maybe the initial infected cells need to be arranged in a way that allows for growth in multiple directions, ensuring that each new cell has two infected neighbors. Perhaps a checkerboard pattern? But a checkerboard pattern with nine cells would have alternating infected and non-infected cells, which might not help because the non-infected cells would need two neighbors.\n\nWait, maybe a better approach is to consider that in order to infect the entire grid, the initial infected cells must form a percolating cluster that can reach all edges, and then use the edges to help infect the remaining cells. But I need to think more concretely.\n\nAlternatively, let's think about the minimum number of cells required to infect the entire grid. Part (b) asks for that, but maybe solving part (b) would help with part (a). If the minimal number is less than or equal to nine, then part (a) is possible. If it's more, then nine isn't enough.\n\nI recall that in similar problems, the minimum number is related to the grid's dimensions. For a 10x10 grid, maybe the minimal number is 10, or perhaps something else. But I need to analyze it.\n\nAlternatively, think about the infection spreading along rows and columns. If you have two infected cells in a row, separated by one cell, then the middle cell can get infected because it has two infected neighbors. Similarly, for columns. So, perhaps a diagonal pattern or spaced out cells can help spread the infection.\n\nWait, here's an idea. If you place infected cells in every other cell in a row, then the cells in between can get infected because they have two infected neighbors. For example, if in row 1, cells (1,1), (1,3), (1,5), (1,7), (1,9) are infected, then the cells (1,2), (1,4), etc., can get infected since each has two infected neighbors (the cells on either side). Similarly, this can spread to the entire row. Then, once a row is fully infected, the adjacent rows can be infected by having two infected neighbors from the row above.\n\nBut to initiate this, you need enough initially infected cells in a row. For example, if you have five infected cells in a row (every other cell), then the remaining cells in the row can be infected in the next step. Then, the next row can be infected by having each cell adjacent to two cells in the infected row. But wait, each cell in the next row is adjacent to only one cell in the infected row (the cell above it). So unless there's another infected cell adjacent horizontally, they won't get infected.\n\nTherefore, maybe a better strategy is to create a 2x2 block of infected cells. A 2x2 block can spread infection to the surrounding cells because each cell adjacent to the block has two infected neighbors. For example, the cell to the right of the 2x2 block is adjacent to two cells in the block. Similarly, the cell below the block is adjacent to two cells. So a 2x2 block can spread in all four directions, creating a cross, which then can spread further.\n\nBut with nine cells, you can have multiple 2x2 blocks. For example, four 2x2 blocks would require 16 cells, which is more than nine. Alternatively, arranging the nine cells in a 3x3 grid, which as we saw before might not reach the edges.\n\nWait,