rhaps we can consider coordinates? Let me set up a coordinate system.\n\nLet me place point D at the origin (0, 0). Then since CD is the base, let\u2019s have CD along the x-axis. Let\u2019s let point C be at (c, 0), point D at (0, 0), point A somewhere above D, and point B somewhere above C. AB is parallel to CD, so AB is also horizontal. Let\u2019s let the height of the trapezoid be h. Then point A would be at (0, h), and point B at (b, h). Then AD is from (0, h) to (0, 0), so AD has length h. Wait, but in a trapezoid, the legs are AD and BC. If AD is vertical, then BC must also be vertical? But then AB and CD would both be horizontal. But in that case, angle MCB would be 150\u00b0, which might complicate things.\n\nWait, maybe AD is not vertical. Hmm, perhaps setting coordinates with D at (0, 0) and C at (d, 0), and AB parallel to CD. Let me assign coordinates more carefully. Let me define coordinate system with D at (0, 0), CD along the x-axis, so C is at (k, 0) for some k > 0. Then AB is parallel to CD, so AB is also horizontal. Let\u2019s say A is at (a, h) and B is at (b, h). Then AD is from (a, h) to (0, 0), so midpoint M of AD is at (a/2, h/2). Point C is at (k, 0). So vector from C to M is (a/2 - k, h/2 - 0) = (a/2 - k, h/2). Vector from C to B is (b - k, h - 0) = (b - k, h). Then angle between vectors CM and CB is 150 degrees. \n\nWait, angle MCB is at point C, so vectors CM and CB. So the angle between CM and CB is 150 degrees. So we can use the dot product formula. The dot product of vectors CM and CB is |CM||CB|cos(theta), where theta is 150 degrees.\n\nFirst, let's write vectors:\n\nVector CM = M - C = (a/2 - k, h/2)\nVector CB = B - C = (b - k, h)\n\nTheir dot product is (a/2 - k)(b - k) + (h/2)(h) = |CM||CB|cos(150\u00b0)\n\nCompute |CM| = sqrt[(a/2 - k)^2 + (h/2)^2] = y (given MC = y)\nCompute |CB| = sqrt[(b - k)^2 + h^2] = x (given BC = x)\n\nTherefore:\n\n(a/2 - k)(b - k) + (h/2)(h) = x y cos(150\u00b0)\n\nBut this seems complicated with many var