use Pell-type equations with square \\( k \\) and constant term 8 are unsolvable.\n\n2. **Multiples of 3**: All multiples of 3 from 3 to 99 (i.e., \\( 3, 6, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 27, 30, 33, 36, 39, 42, 45, 48, 51, 54, 57, 60, 63, 66, 69, 72, 75, 78, 81, 84, 87, 90, 93, 96, 99 \\)) do not have solutions because they fail modular arithmetic checks modulo 3.\n\n3. **Other Excluded Values**: These include numbers where 8 is a quadratic non-residue modulo \\( k \\). These values are identified by checking primes and composites that do not fall into the previous categories. Examples include \\( 5, 10, 11, 13, 19, 22, 29, 35, 37, 43, 44, 55, 59, 65, 67, 77, 83, 85, 95 \\).\n\n### Merging and Sorting Excluded Values:\nCombining all excluded values and removing duplicates, we get a sorted list of excluded \\( k \\):\n\n\\[ 3, 4, 5, 6, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 15, 16, 18, 19, 21, 22, 24, 25, 27, 29, 30, 33, 35, 36, 37, 39, 42, 43, 44, 45, 48, 49, 51, 54, 55, 57, 59, 60, 63, 64, 65, 66, 67, 69, 72, 75, 77, 78, 81, 83, 84, 85, 87, 90, 93, 95, 96, 99 \\]\n\n### Result:\nThe minimum value of \\( k \\) is 3, and the maximum value is 99. The difference between these values is:\n\n\\[\n99 - 3 = \\boxed{96}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": ": \n\nGiven that \\( k \\) is a positive integer not exceeding 99, we need to find the difference between the maximum and minimum possible values of \\( k \\) for which there are no natural numbers \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) such that \\( x^2 - k y^2 = 8 \\).\n\n1. **Understanding the equation:** The equation is given as \\( x^2 - k y^2 = 8 \\). We need to determine the values of \\( k \\) for which this equation has no solutions in natural numbers \\( x \\) and \\( y \\).\n\n2. **Modulo analysis:** Consider the equation modulo 3. For any integer \\( a \\), \\( a^2 \\) modulo 3 can be either 0 or 1 because:\n   \\[\n   0^2 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3}, \\quad 1^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}, \\quad (-1)^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Equation modulo 3:** Suppose \\( k